-- title:
--   Regularity of the cusps of Γ₁(M) for M ∤ 4
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a natural number with $M \nmid 4$, let $\sigma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$, and let $h \in \mathbb{Z}$, where $T = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1\end{pmatrix}$ denotes the standard parabolic generator `ModularGroup.T`. Assume that the conjugate $\sigma T^{h} \sigma^{-1}$ lies in the subgroup of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ generated by $\Gamma_1(M)$ together with $-I$, i.e. in the join (as subgroups) of `CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma1 M` and the subgroup of integral powers of $-1$. Then $\sigma T^{h} \sigma^{-1}$ already lies in $\Gamma_1(M)$ itself, that is, its reduction modulo $M$ has upper-left and lower-right entries congruent to $1$ and lower-left entry congruent to $0$. Thus, for $M$ not dividing $4$, a parabolic element of $\pm\Gamma_1(M)$ conjugate in $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ to a power of $T$ cannot be of the form $-\gamma$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma_1(M)$; the hypothesis $M \nmid 4$ is needed, since the cusp $1/2$ of $\Gamma_1(4)$ is irregular.
--
--   This is the statement that all cusps of $\Gamma_1(M)$ are regular when $M \nmid 4$: a parabolic stabiliser element in $\pm\Gamma_1(M)$ comes from $\Gamma_1(M)$ with the correct sign. It is used in the computation of the order of vanishing of a modular form at a cusp in terms of the width of that cusp, via [`ModularCurve.ord_eq_neg_width_of_order_eq_mul_ord_of_qExpansion_slash`](thm.html#ModularCurve.ord_eq_neg_width_of_order_eq_mul_ord_of_qExpansion_slash).
